      {
        "id": "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Charged and spinning test-body quantities",
        "statement": "Use the smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian background $(M,g,N)$, signature $(-,+,+,+)$, and the distinct chart, observer, congruence, tetrad and worldline meanings of [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. The metric and time orientation are prescribed data, not dynamical variables. A **charged spinning test body** in this definition consists of the following model quantities along a massive worldline $z:I\\to M$:\n\n- $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an open interval whose parameter is proper time $\\tau$ in seconds; $z$ is $C^2$, future-directed and timelike, and $U:=dz/d\\tau$ obeys $g(U,U)=-c^2$. The SI conversion constant is $c>0$ metres per second; in a flat specialization use the affine Minkowski model and the proper-time decomposition of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and [[thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition]].\n- The constant rest-mass parameter $m>0$ has unit kilogram, and the constant charge parameter $q\\in\\mathbb R$ has unit coulomb. They are primitive model parameters; this definition does not derive them from the background field or from another framework.\n- The **kinetic four-momentum** is the derived vector $P:=mU$ along $z$, with unit kilogram metre per second. It is distinct from the potential-dependent covector defined in [[lem-rpm-charge-gauge-boundary]].\n- The **spin or polarization vector** is a $C^1$ tangent vector field $s(\\tau)\\in T_{z(\\tau)}M$ constrained by $g(s,U)=0$, so it lies in the positive-definite instantaneous rest space $U^\\perp$. Its unit is angular momentum, kilogram metre squared per second. This is a classical model vector; no quantization or quantum spin state is defined here.\n- The gyromagnetic ratio $g_s\\in\\mathbb R$ is a dimensionless primitive species parameter. In the instantaneous rest space the model defines the magnetic-moment vector by $\\mu=(g_s q/2m)s$, with unit ampere metre squared. No value $g_s=2$ is assumed, and this constitutive relation is not deduced from Lorentz covariance or classical electromagnetism.\n- The prescribed external electromagnetic field is a $C^1$ alternating covariant two-tensor $F$ on an open region of $M$, independent of the unknown curve. Define $F^\\sharp:T M\\to T M$ by $g(F^\\sharp(v),w)=F(w,v)$. In the flat specialization, a named inertial chart with $x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres uses $F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, matching [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]. On the curved background, $F$ is only a two-form; at an event its components in a chosen orthonormal tetrad use the same algebraic convention. Such a tetrad is a basis field, not a coordinate chart or an observer congruence, as distinguished in [[def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline]]. The components in an orthonormal tetrad have tesla units. This is an explicit curved-background model input. The cited EM supplier is a flat Minkowski definition and does not derive a curved field or curved Maxwell system.\n- On any open potential domain where a $C^2$ covector field $A$ exists, $F=dA$ with components $F_{ab}=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a$ and $A$ has tesla metre components in the stated coordinates. No global potential is assumed, and no Maxwell equation or constitutive law is imposed here.\n\nThis is a test-body model on a fixed background: self-gravity, gravitational backreaction, self-field evaluation, radiation reaction, collisions, electric dipole moments, field-gradient translational forces and higher multipoles are outside this definition. Units follow the framework-local SI conventions of [[def-em-si-conventions]] and do not identify quantities across frameworks.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
          "def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects",
          "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
          "thm-sr-proper-time-and-observer-decomposition",
