      {
        "id": "post-rpm-external-charge-action",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "External-field charged-particle action",
        "statement": "Adopt the following motion principle for the massive charged test body of [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]]. On any finite segment $z:[a,b]\\to O$ in a local potential domain, with $z$ a $C^2$ future-timelike curve and fixed endpoints, the physical trajectories of this idealized model are the stationary points of\n\n$$S[z]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(z',z') }\\,d\\lambda+q\\int_a^b A(z')\\,d\\lambda,$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is an orientation-preserving curve parameter (use seconds for SI evaluation), $A$ is the prescribed $C^2$ external potential, $F=dA$, $m>0$ is constant rest mass, $q$ is constant charge, and $c$ is the SI conversion constant. With $\\lambda=\\tau$ in seconds, coordinates in metres and $A$ in tesla metres, $S$ has units joule seconds. The potential and metric are fixed inputs, independent of the particle path. This is a local action postulate: no global potential is assumed, and a global field equation or global trajectory is not part of the postulate. Its Euler–Lagrange consequence is proved separately in [[pthm-rpm-external-charge-action-equivalence]].\n\nThis model describes a test body: it does not evaluate the body's own field, include self-gravity, or adopt radiation reaction, variable mass or charge, electric dipole forces, or spin-dependent translational forces. The postulate is an adopted model assumption, not a deduction from the electromagnetic field equations or a claim that nature follows this action.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, General Relativity, author-hosted Chapter 1 lecture-note excerpt",
              "url": "https://www.davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/general-relativity/gr1.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 55-page Chapter 1 excerpt; §1.1 fixed-endpoint Euler–Lagrange variation and §1.2.3, especially equation (1.21), printed pp. 8–28. The curved local variation is proved from inspected mathematical suppliers."
            },
            {
              "title": "Eric Poisson, Adam Pound and Ian Vega, The Motion of Point Particles in Curved Spacetime, author manuscript arXiv:1102.0529v3 (2011)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1102.0529v3",
              "locator": "Complete author manuscript, arXiv v3, §5.2.1: curved charged-particle interaction/action and worldline equation, with the singular self-field limitation kept separate from the prescribed external-field model."
            }
