        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "physical_scope": "Adopted dynamics for one massive charged test body on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian background, on finite timelike curve segments contained in a local potential domain. The background metric and external electromagnetic two-form are fixed; the model excludes self-field evaluation, self-gravity, radiation reaction, variable mass or charge, and any global-potential or global-solution claim."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-rpm-bmt-dipole-model",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Classical BMT polarization model",
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the canonical tangent-bundle smooth structure, and let $\\nabla$ be the metric-selected Levi–Civita connection of [[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]. Let $U$ and the prescribed external two-form $F$ be as in [[def-rpm-charge-spin-model]], and let the translational motion obey the adopted action law [[post-rpm-external-charge-action]]. For a $C^1$ spin or polarization vector $s$ along the worldline with $g(s,U)=0$, define $G:=g_s/2$ and $K:=F(s,U)=g(s,F^\\sharp(U))$. Adopt the transport law\n\n$$\\nabla_U s=\\frac{q}{m}\\left[G F^\\sharp(s)-(G-1)\\frac{U}{c^2}K\\right].$$\n\nThis is an ideal classical external-field dipole model with constant $q,m,g_s$. Its flat homogeneous-field specialization is the BMT polarization law in the stated RPM convention. The formula on a curved background, and its use for a spatially varying prescribed field, are separately adopted model assumptions; neither follows from the flat BMT source or from general covariance alone.\n\nThe model excludes an electric dipole moment, field-gradient or Stern–Gerlach translational forces, spin backreaction, radiation reaction, collisions and higher multipoles. The parameter $g_s$ is not fixed to $2$ and no measured value is asserted. Using this classical vector to represent a real quantum particle's polarization is a correspondence assumption; the postulate does not construct quantum spin or claim empirical validation.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-charge-spin-model",
          "post-rpm-external-charge-action",
          "thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
